-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Few-View Collapse**: In few-view settings, NeRFs may collapse to a solution where each camera is covered by a copy of the training image. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{fr}}$ specifically addresses this by penalizing density contained in only one view frustum [S1].
-*   **Depth Scaling**: The division by $D(\mathbf{r})$ in $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ increases the regularizer's strength for geometry close to the camera. If $D(\mathbf{r})$ is near zero, numerical instability may occur; clamping is required [S1].
-*   **Indexing in Distribution Term**: The source formula for $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ contains $\sum_{i,j} w_i w_k$. This is likely a typo for $w_j$. Implementers should verify the symmetry of the term [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Division by Zero:** The expected depth $D(\mathbf{r})$ in $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ can be zero if no weights are accumulated. A small epsilon must be added to the denominator [G].
+*   **Few-View Collapse:** Without $\mathcal{L}_{\text{fr}}$, NeRFs may collapse to copying training images. This loss specifically targets points covered by only one view frustum [S1].
+*   **Index Typo:** The source formula for $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ uses $w_k$ in the pairwise sum. This is interpreted as $w_j$ to match the summation index $j$ [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: The distribution term $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ involves a pairwise summation over samples $i$ and $j$, resulting in $O(N^2)$ complexity per ray. This can be computationally expensive for large $N$.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: Storing intermediate pairwise differences requires $O(N^2)$ memory per ray.
-*   **Performance**: The method provides a strong baseline for NeRF optimization, effectively preventing degenerate solutions in few-view cases [S1].
-*   **Strengths**: Does not require external depth maps or point clouds; relies only on internal volume consistency and multi-view geometry priors [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: The $O(N^2)$ complexity of the distribution term may limit scalability to high-resolution scenes or large numbers of samples. The specific values for $\lambda_{\text{fg}}$ and $\lambda_{\text{fr}}$ are not provided in the source, requiring empirical tuning [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The photometric and foreground losses are linear in the number of rays and samples. The frustum loss is linear. The distribution loss involves a pairwise summation over samples ($O(N^2)$ per ray), which can be computationally expensive for large $N$. Optimizations using vectorized operations or approximations are recommended [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing weights, depths, and frustum counts for all rays. The pairwise distance calculation in $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ requires $O(N^2)$ memory per ray if computed naively, or $O(N)$ with optimized broadcasting [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Effective at preventing degenerate solutions (image copying) in few-view scenarios without requiring external depth maps or point clouds [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The $O(N^2)$ term in $\mathcal{L}_{\text{dist}}$ increases training time. The effectiveness depends on correct scheduling of $\lambda_{\text{dist}}$ [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When training NeRFs with few input views and no external geometric supervision (depth/points) is available [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The Geometric Baseline Regularization was introduced in the context of regularizing Neural Radiance Fields with Denoising Diffusion Models (DiffusioNeRF) [S1]. It was proposed as a strong baseline for NeRF optimization to prevent degenerate solutions, particularly in few-view settings [S1]. The method combines photometric loss with geometric regularizers including foreground completeness, frustum consistency, and distribution compactness [S1].
+Geometric Baseline Regularization was introduced in the context of regularizing NeRFs with Denoising Diffusion Models (DiffusioNeRF) [S1]. It serves as a strong baseline for NeRF optimization, combining photometric and geometric losses to prevent degenerate solutions in few-view settings [S1]. The method is based on the principle that real scenes should have compact depth distributions, rays should be fully absorbed, and density should not be confined to a single view frustum [S1].
 
